  "query_preview": "(Today through Friday)...\nIssued at 249 AM EDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nSurface analysis early this morning shows low pressure in place over \nwestern IL. The low was beginning to push toward Central Indiana, \nproviding cyclonic flow across the area. Radar shows a wave of \nshowers over Illinois, poised to push in to western Central Indiana. \nAloft, water vapor imagery showed an upper low over IA and MO, \npushing east toward Indiana. strong ridging was found over the \nwestern CONUS while weaker ridging was found over the east coast. \nThese ridges will lead to the eventual cutoff of the low. Moisture \nwas plentiful across across Indiana with dew points in the upper 60s.\n\nToday...\n\nModels show the low pressure system pushing into SW Indiana today, \nwith continued broad cyclonic flow across our state.  Forecast \nsoundings shows deep saturation with pwats as high at 1.9 inches \nthis afternoon. CAPE and instability appear minimal. HRRR does not \nshow a shield of rain arriving, but rather spokes or waves passing \nacross the forecast area from time to time. Thus confidence for rain \nwill be high today, but precipitation is more likely to be on and \noff through the day as these spokes pivot. High pops will be used. \nGiven the expected clouds and rain, highs will struggle to reach the \nmid 70s.\n\nTonight through Monday...\n\nLittle overall change is expected the weather tonight through Sunday \nNight. The upper low is expected to be cut off from the main flow \nand stall over SE IND and KY. This will place our area on the north \nside of the low where low cigs and cyclonic flow and light rain and \ndrizzle will be present, especially tonight. Again on Sunday, \ninstability appears limited with little to no CAPE available but \nhigh pwats remain. Thus again, confidence is high for continued on \nand off showers through at least Sunday Night.\n\nOn Monday, rain chances will persist although forecast soundings \nbegin to dry out top down. The low, positioned to the southeast, \nwill allow a cooler and drier flow of air on NE winds. Lower levels \nremain saturated amid cyclonic flow. Thus the heavier rain threat \nshould be diminished by this point but very light rain showers will \nremain possible.\n\nClouds and rain through the period will limit daytime heating, \nallowing for highs mainly the 70s, while low remain in the mid to \nupper 60s. \n\nTuesday through Friday...\n\nAn active weather pattern is expected for much of the work week \nahead. Models suggest the lingering cut off low will finally exit on \nTuesday as a weak trough and sag within the upper flow across the \nCONUS. This will result in a shallow trough aloft over Indiana \nduring this period. This quick flow aloft, across the northern CONUS \nwill bring several embedded short waves across Indiana through the \nwork week providing chances for precipitation. With the lower \nlevels, weak high pressure is suggested to to be moving through the \nGreat Lakes and northern Central Indiana on Tuesday into Wednesday. \nThe northeasterly surface flow may limit rain chances somewhat on \nTuesday and Wednesday, but still some weak forcing will be passing \nwith dew points in the 60s. Forecast soundings at these times show \nmainly a dry column.\n\nHigher confidence for rain will be on Thursday and Friday as the \nhigh drifts to the east which will open up warm and more humid south \nand southwest flow. This in combination with the forcing passing \naloft will necessitate daily chances on Thursday and Friday.",
